termux-exec-package 2.5.0, built into assets/usr/lib/libtermux-exec.so by
scripts/build-termux-exec.sh, with the changes below. The library is
preloaded into the editor's terminals so that a program under the app's own
storage, which SELinux will not execve, is started through
/system/bin/linker64 instead. Upstream's decisions were written for Termux's
layout, where the prefix holds every binary; here the binaries sit in
nativeLibraryDir behind symlinks from usr/bin, and /system's toybox stands in
for tools the prefix does not carry.

  P1  /system executables keep LD_LIBRARY_PATH and LD_PRELOAD. Upstream strips
      both for anything under /system except sh and the two linkers, so
      `env node` and `xargs git` lost the libraries of the program they start
      and were no longer intercepted when they exec'd it.
  P2  A target under the app data directory is realpath()ed and exec'd
      directly when it resolves outside it, so a usr/bin symlink to a
      nativeLibraryDir binary is not started through the linker, which would
      make the linker its /proc/self/exe.
  P3  /usr/bin/X and /bin/X fall back to /system/bin/X when $TERMUX__PREFIX/bin/X
      is absent, so `#!/bin/sh` and `#!/usr/bin/env` scripts run without a
      copy of every toybox tool in the prefix.
  P4  A manual `linker64 <absolute payload>` sets TERMUX_EXEC__PROC_SELF_EXE to
      that payload instead of stripping an inherited value, so what the exec
      trampoline and the toolchain launchers do by hand keeps a self-locating
      payload working.
  P5  The /system fallback of P3 also applies when TERMUX__PREFIX is unset and
      the compiled-in default does not exist, the one case P3 alone made
      worse: /bin/sh ran with no preload and failed with it.

Each changed file gains a first-line notice that it was modified, which the
Apache-2.0 licence (section 4(b)) asks of a redistributed modification.

Measured on API 33 and 36 emulators, 2026-09-22/23. Applies with `patch -p1`
to the pristine 2.5.0 tree, `--dry-run` included; the build script applies it
to a fresh tree on every run and stops if a hunk does not fit.

